---
/**
 * hospitality / HeroBK — "Villa Marindu"'s arrival.
 *
 * ROLE. The page's opening statement and its only h1. A full-bleed photograph
 * fills the section, a bottom-weighted gradient scrim sits over it, and the copy
 * stack — brass eyebrow, Prata headline, lede, two actions — is bottom-aligned
 * inside it. Lifted verbatim from the HERO element of "Villa Marindu
 * Landing.dc.html": min-height 92vh, align-items flex-end, 92px of floor,
 * h1 clamp(46px,6.6vw,96px)/1.02 at 20ch in --bone-hi with an ITALIC BRASS <em>,
 * lede 18.5px/1.7 in --grey-2 at 50ch, actions 13px apart.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (each path is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts, so each is live in the sidebar AND inline):
 *   hero.image          — data-image-field, the full-bleed background photograph
 *   hero.kicker         — the eyebrow (.kick)
 *   hero.headline       — the h1, set:html so an owner <em> takes the brass italic
 *   hero.headlineLines.${i} — the SAME h1, when hero.headline holds nothing but
 *                         the derived business name; see THE HEADLINE below
 *   hero.sub            — the lede
 *   hero.cta1.text + data-href-field hero.cta1.href — the brass button
 *   hero.cta2.text + data-href-field hero.cta2.href — the outlined button
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the photograph's accessible name — layout.businessName and nothing else
 *   · both button hrefs, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBK
 *     actually renders
 *   · which of the two headline paths the h1 binds to
 *   · the flat variant (no photograph ⇒ no 92vh), below
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("A whole villa, and no one else in it.", "See the villa", "Private villa ·
 *     El Nido, Palawan") are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A real
 *     business gets an arrival that says only what that business said, so every
 *     guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no eyebrow over nothing, no empty action row,
 *     and no scrim painted over a photograph that was never uploaded.
 *   · hero.meta1 / hero.meta2 / hero.trustLine — schema-owned, but this design
 *     has no element for them and the TRUST band directly below carries the
 *     proof figures instead. (meta1 in particular used to default to a
 *     five-star claim; there is no honest place for it here.)
 *   · hero.tag / hero.photos.* — not declared by the schema. A data-field on a
 *     path no sidebar input owns looks editable and focuses nothing.
 *   · The design's `pointer-events:none` on the copy stack, and `pointer-events:
 *     auto` on the action row. That pair exists so the DESIGNER can click the
 *     image-slot lying behind the words. In the admin editor it would make the
 *     h1, the eyebrow and the lede unclickable — inline editing dead, text
 *     unselectable — so it is dropped. Nothing sits behind the copy here that a
 *     visitor needs to reach.
 *   · .reveal. This section is above the fold; it is painted, not animated in.
 *   · An aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would turn it into a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote ("Hero"), which is studio jargon; the h1
 *     already titles the region.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE, and why one heading reads two paths. This design's headline is
 * ONE flowing sentence whose closing phrase falls into brass italic — not a
 * stack of lines — so the h1 is hero.headline rendered through set:html and the
 * emphasis is an <em> the OWNER types. Nothing derives the italic: there is no
 * honest way to choose which phrase of someone else's sentence should turn
 * brass.
 * The catch is WHICH FIELD actually holds the owner's headline, because two
 * layers upstream disagree:
 *   · lib/derive-content-defaults.ts auto-fills hero.headline with the BARE
 *     BUSINESS NAME on every named site (`headline: name || undefined`), and
 *     lib/astro-builder.ts's mergeShallow lets that derived value stand
 *     wherever the owner left the field unset.
 *   · lib/services/groq.service.ts — the extraction that writes the copy for a
 *     real submission — emits hero.headlineLines and NEVER hero.headline.
 * So "always prefer hero.headline" prints the business name and drops the
 * authored headline on the floor for exactly the sites that have one. The rule
 * here is therefore: hero.headline wins whenever it is AUTHORED, meaning
 * anything other than the business name the derive layer fills in. When it is
 * that auto-fill (or empty), the surviving headlineLines rows win — flowed
 * INLINE inside the same h1, because this design's headline is a sentence and
 * not a stack — each carrying its ORIGINAL array index, so dropping a blank
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 * The field a click opens is always the field the visible text came from:
 * binding hero.headlineLines.0 to text that arrived as hero.headline (or the
 * reverse) aims an inline edit at a slot the sidebar is not showing. Both paths
 * are declared by components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts ('Headline (single
 * line)' and the 'Headline lines' list), so neither is a dead hook.
 *
 * SECTION GATE. The arrival renders when it has something to SAY or SHOW: a
 * headline, a lede, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT count the
 * eyebrow (derive-content-defaults builds one from city + trade for sites that
 * have neither name, tagline nor upload — a 92vh black rectangle carrying one
 * tracked word is exactly the "heading over an empty section" failure), and it
 * does NOT count the two buttons (derive sets cta1/cta2 text UNCONDITIONALLY,
 * so counting either would make the gate a no-op that always passes).
 * FOR REVIEW: this is the one section in the family whose gate counts the
 * headline, because here the headline IS the body — it is the document's only
 * h1, and a hero dropped for having "only" an h1 takes the page's heading with
 * it and opens the site on the trust band.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH ⇒ NO 92vh. The design's arrival is a photograph with words at
 * its foot. With no upload, 92vh of empty ground above bottom-aligned copy is a
 * layout with a hole in it, so the flat variant drops the min-height and pads
 * top and bottom like an ordinary section. The scrim goes with the photo: it
 * exists only to hold the copy legible over one.
 *
 * COLOUR / CONTRAST (R1 — no literal hex in this file, only PageBK's tokens):
 *   --bone-hi headline 16.5:1, --grey-2 lede 11.1:1, --accent eyebrow and italic
 *   6.62:1, all measured on --night. Over the photograph the design's own scrim
 *   carries them: rgba(--scrim-rgb,.94) at the floor where the copy sits, so the
 *   text never rides a bare image. The scrim is the design's gradient verbatim.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .vm-arrive, NOT
 * .hero — buildOverrideCss force-paints `.hero .btn-ghost` with !important and
 * would strand a label here. Buttons are .btn + .btn--fill / .btn--line (double
 * dash) for the same reason. Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow /
 * .btn-light / .nav-cta / .trust / .testi / .cta-band in this family.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;

// Anchor guard, copied from the BJ sibling and re-pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes '#visit' for hero.cta1 and
// '#services' for hero.cta2; '#visit' is an id no page in this family renders,
// so the primary button scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href against
// what PageBK actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this
// design carries the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the
// target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass
// through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBK — and with the copies
// in HeaderBK, CtaBandBK and FooterBK. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the copies
// must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['about', 'services', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  visit: 'location', contact: 'location',
  rooms: 'services', amenities: 'services',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};

// Display fallbacks only — the HOOK below is always hero.image, the path the
// sidebar's image picker writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an
// apostrophe from closing the url('') early and killing the declaration.
const heroImage = String(hero.image || (hero.photos && hero.photos[0]) || (content.photos && content.photos[0]) || '');
const heroImageCss = heroImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const kicker = hero.kicker || '';

// THE HEADLINE — full reasoning in the doc comment. hero.headline is this
// design's field; the rows take over only when hero.headline is nothing but the
// business name derive-content-defaults auto-filled (or is empty), which is the
// shape every AI-written submission arrives in.
const bizName = String(layout?.businessName ?? '').trim();
const headline = String(hero.headline ?? '').trim();
const lineRows: Array<{ text: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(hero.headlineLines) ? hero.headlineLines : [])
  .map((t: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(t ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { text: string }) => l.text.trim() !== '');
// …and only when they SAY something hero.headline does not. On the ordinary
// named site both hold the same one line, and the single-line hook keeps it.
const useRows =
  lineRows.length > 0 &&
  (headline === '' || headline === bizName) &&
  lineRows.map((l) => l.text.trim()).join(' ') !== headline;
const hasHeadline = useRows || headline !== '';

// ── THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// This design's h1 is two-tone: bone, then a brass italic tail. It got there
// because the designer typed <em> into their own markup. No owner is going to
// do that from a text box, so on every real site the headline rendered one flat
// colour and the most recognisable move in the design never shipped.
//
// When the owner has NOT marked emphasis themselves, the tail is derived from
// their OWN sentence and adds not one word:
//   • more than one line          -> the last line
//   • a comma, dash, colon or semicolon -> everything after the LAST one
//   • anything else               -> nothing at all
//
// That last case is deliberate. A single clause is a single clause, and
// splitting inside one would lean on a word the owner never leaned on — the
// design's own headline ("A whole villa, and no one else in it.") breaks at a
// comma precisely because that is where its sentence turns.
//
// An explicit <em> always wins: this only fills a silence.
const hasOwnEm = (v: string): boolean => /<em[\s>]/i.test(v);
const emphasise = (raw: string): string => {
  if (!raw || hasOwnEm(raw)) return raw;
  const lines = raw.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (lines.length > 1) {
    const tail = lines.pop() as string;
    return lines.join(" ") + " <em>" + tail + "</em>";
  }
  // The LAST break wins, so a sentence with two commas emphasises its close
  // rather than its middle. The lookahead keeps a trailing comma from matching.
  const re = /[,;:\u2014\u2013]\s+(?=\S)/g;
  let cut = -1;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null) cut = m.index + m[0].length;
  if (cut <= 0 || cut >= raw.length) return raw;
  return raw.slice(0, cut) + "<em>" + raw.slice(cut) + "</em>";
};
const headlineHtml = emphasise(headline);
// The rows path marks its LAST row instead, and only when no row is already
// carrying the owner's own <em>.
const rowsOwnEm = lineRows.some((l) => hasOwnEm(l.text));

const sub = hero.sub || '';
const cta1Text = hero.cta1?.text || '';
const cta1Href = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#location');
const cta2Text = hero.cta2?.text || '';
const cta2Href = onPage(hero.cta2?.href, '#gallery');

// The photograph's accessible name is the owner's own words or nothing. With no
// business name there is no honest name for the picture, so it leaves the
// accessibility tree instead of announcing a phrase I invented. Built as one
// spread object so a conditional attribute can never end up beside a literal.
const photoA11y: Record<string, string> = bizName
  ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': bizName }
  : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };

const hasArrival = !!(hasHeadline || sub || heroImage);
---
{hasArrival && (
<section class={heroImage ? 'vm-arrive' : 'vm-arrive vm-arrive--flat'}>
  {/* Layer 0 — the photograph. No upload ⇒ no layer at all, rather than a
      tinted rectangle standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
  {heroImage && (
    <div class="plate vm-shot">
      <div
        class="ph vm-ph"
        data-image-field="hero.image"
        style={`background-image:url('${heroImageCss}');`}
        {...photoA11y}
      ></div>
    </div>
  )}
  {/* Layer 1 — the design's scrim, verbatim. Purely presentational, and it only
      exists where there is a photograph to darken. */}
  {heroImage && <div class="vm-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>}

  {/* Layer 2 — the copy stack.

      TWO ELEMENTS, NOT ONE. .wrap is the page's centring container
      (width: min(1280px, 92vw); margin-inline: auto) and .vm-copy is the panel.
      Putting a max-width on the same element that carries margin-inline:auto
      centres the PANEL in the viewport instead of seating it at the wrap's left
      edge — the panel drifted to the middle of the page and stopped lining up
      with the wordmark above it. The container centres; the panel sits left
      inside it. */}
  <div class="wrap vm-copy-wrap">
  <div class="vm-copy">
    {kicker && <span class="kick vm-kick" data-field="hero.kicker">{kicker}</span>}
    {!useRows && headline !== '' && <h1 class="vm-h1" data-field="hero.headline" set:html={headlineHtml}></h1>}
    {useRows && (
      <h1 class="vm-h1">
        {lineRows.map((l, k) => (
          <span class:list={['vm-ln', !rowsOwnEm && k === lineRows.length - 1 && 'vm-ln--em']}><span data-field={`hero.headlineLines.${l.i}`} set:html={l.text}></span>{k === lineRows.length - 1 ? '' : ' '}</span>
        ))}
      </h1>
    )}
    {sub && <p class="vm-lede" data-field="hero.sub">{sub}</p>}
    {(cta1Text || cta2Text) && (
      <div class="vm-ctas">
        {cta1Text && (
          <a class="btn btn--fill vm-cta-lg" href={cta1Href} data-field="hero.cta1.text" data-href-field="hero.cta1.href">{cta1Text}</a>
        )}
        {cta2Text && (
          <a class="btn btn--line" href={cta2Href} data-field="hero.cta2.text" data-href-field="hero.cta2.href">{cta2Text}</a>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBK's token vocabulary
     (--night --bone-hi --grey-2 --accent --scrim-rgb --disp --sans) and from its
     shared primitives (.wrap .kick .plate .ph .btn .btn--fill .btn--line).
     There is no literal hex in this file.
     Every rule below is a class selector, which out-specifies the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BK"] section` and its :where()-wrapped primitives — that is
     how the 92vh arrival overrides the shared section padding without
     !important. */

  /* Design: position:relative; min-height:92vh; display:flex;
     align-items:flex-end; overflow:hidden; floor 92px. */
  .vm-arrive {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 92vh;
    display: flex;
    /* The panel sits high, under the rail, with the photograph carrying the
       space beneath it. */
    align-items: flex-start;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The design's floor is a flat 92px — not a clamp. It sits on the SECTION
       rather than on the copy block because the copy is the bottom-aligned flex
       item, which puts the same 92px between the last line and the edge. The
       narrow reductions live in the 900 query below. */
    padding: clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px) 0 92px;
  }
  /* No photograph ⇒ no 92vh of empty ground. See the doc comment. */
  .vm-arrive--flat {
    min-height: 0;
    padding: clamp(48px, 6.4vw, 84px) 0 clamp(52px, 6.6vw, 88px);
  }

  /* The photograph fills the section edge to edge: no radius, no floor height,
     no reserved minimum — it is a ground, not a framed plate. */
  .vm-shot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class, so `.vm-ph` alone would lose to it.
     Restating cover/center here costs nothing and means the photograph cannot
     letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. No colour is restated — that
     stays in the primitive, where the theme engine keeps control of it. */
  .vm-shot .vm-ph {
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* The design's gradient, unchanged: heavy at the floor where the words sit,
     open through the middle, a little weight again at the top so the sticky
     header keeps its edge. rgba(11,14,13,…) is --scrim-rgb. */
  .vm-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .94) 4%,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .35) 52%,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .55) 100%
    );
  }

  /* No width here — .wrap owns the measure, and a width on this class would
     out-specify it and blow the copy out to the full viewport. */
  /* ── THE READABILITY PANEL ────────────────────────────────────────────
     Copy that sits on a photograph gets a ground. Every other section in
     this template is type on a flat colour and needs nothing; the two
     photo-backed ones — this and the closing band — put their copy in a
     bordered near-black panel, which is how the design renders and the only
     way a 96px serif stays readable over a picture the owner chose and we
     have never seen.

     The scrim stays: it grades the whole photograph so the panel is not a
     bright rectangle floating on an unrelated image. The panel then carries
     the text.

     Values: the design's own scrim colour at .92, its hairline, its 3px
     corner. The width is the copy measure it already had plus the padding,
     so the panel wraps the text rather than the text being re-flowed to fit
     a new box. */
  /* The container only carries the measure and the centring, both from the
     shared .wrap primitive. It must NOT be given a width of its own. */
  .vm-copy-wrap { min-width: 0; }

  .vm-copy {
    max-width: min(760px, 100%);
    /* Explicit: the panel seats at the container's left edge, never centred.
       margin-inline:auto on an ancestor is what put it in the middle. */
    margin-inline: 0 auto;
    background-color: rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .92);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: clamp(30px, 4.2vw, 56px);
  }
  .vm-copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* Every row here is guarded away when its value is empty, so whichever row
     ends up last must not leave its own bottom margin sitting on top of the
     design's 92px floor (a hero with no actions would otherwise stand 126px
     off the edge). Costs nothing in the full design, where the last child is
     the action row and carries no margin. */
  .vm-copy > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .vm-h1 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(46px, 6.6vw, 96px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin: .22em 0 .3em;
    color: var(--bone-hi);
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
  /* THE SIGNATURE. Prata ships one weight and no italic, so this synthesises an
     oblique — which is what the design does too. :global() because Astro cannot
     scope markup that arrived through set:html. */
  .vm-h1 :global(em) { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
  /* Rows path only (hero.headlineLines). They FLOW as one sentence rather than
     stacking, because this design's h1 is a sentence — the wrap comes from the
     20ch measure, exactly as it does on the single-line path. The word space
     between rows is a real text node in the markup, not a faked margin. */
  .vm-ln { display: inline; }
  /* The derived tail on the rows path. Same treatment as an owner-typed <em>,
     declared separately because the rows are real elements this file owns and
     do not need :global(). */
  .vm-ln--em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }

  .vm-lede {
    font-size: 18.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--grey-2);
    max-width: 50ch;
    margin: 0 0 34px;
  }

  .vm-ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 13px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* The design's btnLg is the shared button at 34px of side padding rather than
     32px — the only metric in which the arrival's primary differs from every
     other brass button on the page. */
  .vm-cta-lg { padding-left: 34px; padding-right: 34px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .vm-arrive { min-height: 78vh; padding-bottom: clamp(44px, 6vw, 72px); }
    /* Restated AFTER the rule above, in every query that touches .vm-arrive:
       the flat variant matches .vm-arrive too, so a later-in-source min-height
       or padding-bottom would otherwise reach it and undo the whole point of
       the variant. */
    .vm-arrive--flat { min-height: 0; padding-bottom: clamp(52px, 6.6vw, 88px); }
    .vm-h1 { font-size: clamp(38px, 7.4vw, 64px); max-width: 22ch; }
    .vm-lede { font-size: 17px; max-width: 46ch; margin-bottom: 28px; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
       anywhere, and a long uppercase label wraps inside a full-width button
       instead of widening the row. */
    .vm-arrive { min-height: 74vh; }
    .vm-arrive--flat { min-height: 0; }
    .vm-kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .vm-h1 { font-size: clamp(31px, 9vw, 44px); max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .vm-lede { font-size: 16px; max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin-bottom: 24px; }
    .vm-ctas { gap: 10px; }
    .vm-ctas .btn { flex: 1 1 100%; white-space: normal; text-align: center; }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's 520 padding: without this the class above keeps the
       primary at 34px while the secondary drops to 24px. */
    .vm-cta-lg { padding-left: 24px; padding-right: 24px; }
  }
</style>
